PLACES=[
# ---- Marine on St. Croix and Arcola ----
N('marine-mill-site','Marine Mill site','Marine on St. Croix','mn','industry','ruins',45.198244,-92.768195,'site','public','walk','','1839–1895',
  'Lewis Judd and David Hone of Marine, Illinois picked this stream mouth in 1838, and their Marine Lumber Company cut its first lumber here on August 24, 1839: the first commercial sawmill in what became Minnesota. A bigger water-wheel mill replaced it in 1852, burned in 1863, was rebuilt in 1866 and remodelled in 1873. The business failed in 1885 and was dismantled by 1895; the state bought the site in 1971–72 and it passed to the Minnesota Historical Society.',
  'The stone walls of the 1873 sawmill and its water-wheel pit, seen from an overlook at the end of an open lawn, and a short interpretive trail past the faint foundations of two other mill buildings; the stream that drove the mill still runs through the site (Wikipedia). MNHS says its trails are under half a mile of packed gravel on level ground. A 1950 highway marker nearby shows the millstones of an 1857 flour mill.',
  'Where Minnesota\'s lumber industry began, in a village that still looks like New England.',
  accn='Minnesota Historical Society site, run with the City of Marine on St. Croix. Judd Street, just off Highway 95; MNHS says there is a free parking lot at the site and admission is free (651-433-3636). Its hours page lists no hours: hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Marine Mill'),['MNHS: Marine Mill, hours and admission','https://www.mnhs.org/marinemill/visit/hours'],['MNHS: directions and parking','https://www.mnhs.org/marinemill/visit/directions'],['HMdb: Marine Mills flour mill marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=28509']],
  brick='no'),
N('marine-township-hall-stone-house','Marine Township Hall (Stone House Museum)','Marine on St. Croix','mn','civic','standing',45.20018,-92.77183,'site','view','walk','','1872',
  'Built in 1872 as the township meeting hall and jail, on land given by Orange Walker of the mill company, by the local mason Gustaf Carlson from stone quarried and cut near the village. After the township was abolished in 1895 it served as a school, a store and a community centre. The Historic American Buildings Survey measured it in 1934 as a fine example of the stonework of Minnesota\'s Swedish settlers, and since 1963 it has been a museum, first run by the Women\'s Civic Club of Marine (HMdb marker text).',
  'A small hall of local cut stone on 5th Street, with a 1968 Minnesota Historical Society marker in front. A few streets away the 1888 Village Hall is, by a 2017 marker\'s account, the oldest town hall in Minnesota still used for government, and the Marine General Store carries on the lumber company store of 1849 in a building of the 1870s; the whole village centre is a National Register district. Interior not checked.',
  'A Swedish mason\'s township hall and jail of 1872.',
  accn='5th Street, 0.1 mile north of Maple Street (County Road 4). Museum opening hours not checked; the Washington County Historical Society\'s Marine page names the "1872 Stone House Museum" but gives no hours. View from the street at other times. Street parking and a lot on 5th Street.',
  links=[['HMdb: The Marine Township Hall','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=45939'],['HMdb: Marine on St. Croix village marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=147264'],['WCHS: Marine on St. Croix',WCHS+'?p=726'],W('Marine on St. Croix, Minnesota')],
  th=[45.19975,-92.77248],thn='Parking lot on 5th Street, Marine on St. Croix',brick='no'),
N('arcola-mills','Arcola Mills: Mower House and sawmill chimney','Stillwater Township','mn','industry','standing',45.138094,-92.751689,'site','ask','walk','','1847–1870s',
  'Brothers Martin and John Mower, from Maine, started one of the first sawmills on the St. Croix here in 1847 and built the town of Arcola around it for their workers: store, school, boarding house and shops. The mill went over to steam in 1856 and closed by the 1870s. The Greek Revival house of 1847, which Wikipedia calls the third-oldest wood-frame house standing in Minnesota (the foundation itself says the oldest timber-frame home), stood empty until Henry and Katharine Van Meier bought it in the 1930s; her will set up the Arcola Mills Historic Foundation, which restored it.',
  'The two-storey clapboard house with corner pilasters and pedimented gables, and a few hundred feet north a stone chimney of the sawmill, still standing beside a derelict 20th-century cottage (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'A lumber baron\'s 1847 house and the chimney of his mill, on a quiet bend of the St. Croix.',
  accn='Private nonprofit land (Arcola Mills Historic Foundation, on Arcola Trail North). The foundation\'s visit page says it is normally open May to October with hiking trails round the property, but that it has been closed for two years for habitat restoration under a DNR Legacy grant and hopes to reopen soon. Ask the foundation first (its contact is on the visit page). OpenStreetMap maps a lot on Arcola Trail North about 130 m south-west, but it could not be made out on the aerial.',
  links=[W('Arcola Mills'),['Arcola Mills Historic Foundation: visit','https://www.arcolamills.org/visit']],
  brick='no'),
N('st-croix-boom-site','St. Croix Boom Site','Stillwater Township','mn','industry','traces',45.08505,-92.78573,'area','public','walk','','1856–1914',
  'From 1856 to 1914 logs floated down the St. Croix were caught here by booms chained across the river, sorted by the owners\' marks stamped in their ends, and made up into rafts for the mills downstream. Isaac Staples\' Stillwater syndicate moved the boom here from near Taylors Falls in 1856; by its close on June 12, 1914 it had handled over 15.5 billion feet of logs, and it was so powerful it was called "The Octopus". It became a roadside park in the 1930s and a National Historic Landmark in 1966.',
  'Wikipedia says virtually nothing of the boom\'s works is left. What there is to see: the river where the pens lay, a shrine-type limestone marker at the highway wayside (its plaque replaced with a replica in 2011), a geological tablet on Lake St. Croix, and interpretive panels on the plaza at the south end of the north lot. DNR describes a half-mile trail along the river linking the markers, and a large staircase down to the beach. The superintendent\'s house and barn of about 1885 stand nearby but are not part of the wayside.',
  'Fifteen billion feet of white pine passed through pens on this stretch of river.',
  accn='St. Croix Boom Site State Wayside (Minnesota DNR), 26 acres on Highway 95 about 2.5 miles north of Stillwater. DNR lists a picnic area, shoreline fishing, a half-mile river trail and a seasonal toilet; reaching the beach means a large staircase. Hours not checked. The boat landing here is the start of the National Park Service\'s no-personal-watercraft stretch of the St. Croix.',
  links=[W('St. Croix Boom Site'),['Minnesota DNR: state waysides','https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_parks/waysides/index.html'],['HMdb: St. Croix Boom Site marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=44673'],['HMdb: interpretive panel on the plaza','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=131175'],['NPS: boating on the St. Croix','https://www.nps.gov/sacn/planyourvisit/boating.htm']],
  th=[45.08565,-92.78491],thn='Boom Site wayside lot, Highway 95',brick='?'),
# ---- Stillwater ----
N('wardens-house-museum-stillwater','Warden\'s House Museum (Minnesota Territorial Prison)','Stillwater','mn','inst','standing',45.061632,-92.8075,'exact','open','walk','','1853–1914',
  'The house of the wardens of the Minnesota Territorial Prison, built with the prison in 1851–53 on the bluff above the ravine where the walls stood; thirteen wardens lived here until the prison moved to Bayport in 1914. The prison itself was mostly pulled down in 1936; its 1884–98 twine-factory buildings survived until an arson fire on September 3, 2002, and condominiums (Terra Springs) now stand on the site. The Washington County Historical Society has kept the house as a museum since 1941.',
  'A two-storey house of local limestone in an early Greek Revival style, with 1870 frame additions at the back; the front porch is a later replacement. Fourteen rooms restored to the late 1800s and early 1900s, with prison exhibits and things belonging to the Younger brothers of the James-Younger gang (Wikipedia, WCHS). Inside only on guided tours. Nothing of the prison is left in the ravine below.',
  'The only building left of Minnesota\'s territorial prison, where the Younger brothers did their time.',
  accn='Washington County Historical Society, 602 Main Street North. WCHS gives the hours as Thursday to Sunday, guided tours at 1, 2, 3 and 4 p.m., closed Monday to Wednesday; adults $10, ages 6–17 $3 (651-439-5956). The page does not say whether it closes for winter: check before going.',
  links=[W('Warden\'s House Museum'),W('Minnesota Territorial Prison'),['WCHS: Warden\'s House Museum',WCHS+'?p=4190']],
  th=[45.06102,-92.80585],thn='Lot on Laurel Street East, Stillwater',brick='no'),
N('staples-sawmill-stillwater','Isaac Staples\' sawmill powerhouse','Stillwater','mn','industry','standing',45.059927,-92.807535,'site','open','walk','','1850',
  'Sawyer and Heaton built a stone powerhouse here in 1850 for the second sawmill in Stillwater. Isaac Staples bought the mill in 1869 and ran it as the St. Croix Lumber Mills until the 1880s; it then became a flour mill and, from 1888, the Stillwater Manufacturing Company\'s door and sash works. An 1894 fire destroyed every wooden building and left only the limestone powerhouse, beside which a four-storey metal-sided factory went up in 1900.',
  'The 1850 limestone powerhouse with its smokestack, and the 1900 metal-clad factory beside it, now a shopping building with flats upstairs (Wikipedia; search results show it as Staples Mill Antiques). Interior not checked.',
  'The one industrial building left of Stillwater\'s great lumber baron.',
  accn='Privately owned shops on Main Street North; shop hours not checked. Look from Main Street at any time. Downtown lots nearby.',
  links=[W('Isaac Staples\' Sawmill')],
  th=[45.05950,-92.80675],thn='Lot on Main Street North, Stillwater',brick='no'),
N('washington-county-historic-courthouse','Washington County Historic Courthouse','Stillwater','mn','civic','standing',45.05152,-92.80741,'exact','open','walk','','1870',
  'Designed by Augustus Knight of St. Paul in the Italianate style and finished in 1870, on Zion\'s Hill above the lumber town, for $60,000. It was the seat of the county for over a century, until the new government centre opened in 1975, and was nominated to the Register as Minnesota\'s oldest functioning courthouse. The county has restored it as a place for events, tours and history.',
  'A brick courthouse on a limestone foundation, with a prominent dome and cupola (Wikipedia). The county says the lobby, exhibits and the historic jail are open to visitors; guided history tours are offered.',
  'Minnesota\'s oldest standing courthouse, jail and all, on the hill over Stillwater.',
  accn='Run by Washington County. The county gives the public hours of the lobby, exhibits and historic jail as Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. and weekends 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., closed most county holidays but open Memorial Day and Veterans Day. Lot on Walnut Street beside it.',
  links=[W('Washington County Courthouse (Minnesota)'),['Washington County: Historic Courthouse','https://washingtoncountymn.gov/3472']],
  th=[45.05140,-92.80773],thn='Lot on Walnut Street West',brick='yes'),
N('stillwater-lift-bridge','Stillwater Lift Bridge','Stillwater','mn','landmark','standing',45.056389,-92.803333,'site','public','walk','','1931',
  'A vertical-lift bridge of seven steel truss spans built in 1931 to replace a 1910 swing bridge, the cost split between Minnesota and Wisconsin. Its lift span is a Waddell & Harrington type, one of six built in the two states and one of three left. It carried Highways 36 and 64 until the St. Croix Crossing opened downriver on August 2, 2017, and was then restored for people on foot and on bicycles.',
  'The steel trusses and the towers of the lift span, from the bridge deck or the riverbank; the span still rises for boats (Wikipedia shows it raised in 2013; the present lifting schedule was not checked).',
  'A 1931 lift bridge that now carries walkers between two states.',
  accn='St. Croix County, Wisconsin says the 4.7-mile Loop Trail, which crosses both the St. Croix Crossing and the Lift Bridge, is open year-round to people on foot and on bicycles. Downtown lots near the west end.',
  links=[W('Stillwater Bridge (St. Croix River)'),['St. Croix County: Loop Trail','https://sccwi.gov/963/Loop-Trail']],
  th=[45.05419,-92.80382],thn='Lot by the Loop Trail, downtown Stillwater',brick='no'),
N('stillwater-overlook','Stillwater Overlook (Lake St. Croix Overlook)','Oak Park Heights','mn','landmark','standing',45.040524,-92.795795,'site','public','drive','','1936–1937',
  'A roadside overlook built in 1936–37 by young workers of the National Youth Administration for the Minnesota Highway Department, to a design by the landscape architect Arthur R. Nichols. The NYA crews were also pulling down the old territorial prison two miles north, and its salvaged limestone went into this wall. MnDOT counts it among the most refined and intact of the Depression-era waysides.',
  'A curving overlook wall about 140 feet square of tan, roughly cut limestone, stone and concrete benches, a stone culvert, stone-curbed parking and an aluminium "Lake St. Croix" plaque, looking over the river valley (MnDOT). The stone is from the prison.',
  'The territorial prison\'s stones, rebuilt into a view.',
  accn='Owned by MnDOT. East side of Lookout Trail near 63rd Street North, by the junction of Highways 36 and 95; it has its own small paved lot. Hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Stillwater Overlook'),['MnDOT: Stillwater (Lake St. Croix) Overlook','https://www.dot.state.mn.us/roadsides/historic-properties/lake-st-croix.html']],
  brick='no'),
N('log-cabin-oak-park-heights','Log Cabin roadhouse (Phil\'s Tara Hideaway)','Oak Park Heights','mn','hotel','standing',45.03527,-92.801811,'site','open','drive','','1932',
  'Whitey Lynch\'s Chicken Shack opened here in 1929 on a lonely stretch of highway outside Stillwater. After a fire (Wikipedia says May 1932, the restaurant says 1931) it was rebuilt as a log cabin, a nod to the logging past, and it ran as a roadhouse in the last years of Prohibition, rumoured to serve liquor and shelter gangsters. It was renamed Club Tara Hideaway in 1946 and has been Phil\'s Tara Hideaway since 1997.',
  'The log-cabin building of the early 1930s, still a restaurant. Interior not checked.',
  'A Prohibition roadhouse that still serves dinner.',
  accn='Private restaurant on Highway 36 at Oak Park Heights; hours not checked (tarahideaway.com). Wikipedia\'s 2017 photograph shows accessible parking spaces at the door.',
  links=[W('Log Cabin (Oak Park Heights, Minnesota)'),['Phil\'s Tara Hideaway: about','https://www.tarahideaway.com/about-us']],conf='mixed',
  brick='some'),
N('point-douglas-stone-arch-bridge','Point Douglas–St. Louis River Road stone arch bridge','Stillwater Township','mn','landmark','standing',45.075556,-92.828889,'site','view','walk','','1863–1865',
  'A limestone arch over Brown\'s Creek on the military road from Point Douglas to Lake Superior, one of Minnesota\'s first road projects. Sources disagree on who built it: Wikipedia says the county rejected Hanley and Curtis\'s work of 1863 and accepted one built by Commissioner William Willim in October 1865, while the DNR\'s trail panel credits Hanly and Curtis in 1863 and calls it Minnesota\'s oldest remaining stone arch bridge (Wikipedia puts it second, after one at Mendota). The road moved to a wooden bridge in 1891; a mill built on it in 1905 was torn down in 1927.',
  'A semi-elliptical limestone arch about 20 feet long and 17 wide, rising 12 feet over the creek (HMdb panel), seen from Brown\'s Creek State Trail near milepost 2, west of Stonebridge Trail North (County Road 55).',
  'A territorial military road\'s stone bridge, seen from a rail trail.',
  accn='The bridge is privately owned and the trail panel says access is not permitted and the bridge is unstable: view it from Brown\'s Creek State Trail (Minnesota DNR), a paved former railroad grade. DNR names trail parking on Laurel Street East in downtown Stillwater, in Brown\'s Creek Nature Preserve south of McKusick Road and Neal Avenue, and in Grant near Lake Masterman. Trail hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Point Douglas–St. Louis River Road Bridge'),['HMdb: Bridging Brown\'s Creek (DNR panel)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=232817'],['Minnesota DNR: Brown\'s Creek State Trail','https://www.mndnr.gov/state_trails/browns_creek/index.html']],conf='mixed',
  brick='no'),
N('gateway-trail-iron-bridge','Gateway Trail Iron Bridge (Bridge 5721)','Stillwater Township','mn','landmark','standing',45.104505,-92.864902,'site','public','walk','','1873',
  'A wrought-iron camelback (MnDOT says Parker) truss, built in 1873 to carry Main Street in Sauk Centre over the Sauk River, before steel took over for bridges. It was stored, put up again in 1937 on Highway 65 over the Little Fork River in Koochiching County, and, too weak for logging trucks, taken down in 2009 and set up here in May 2011 to carry the Gateway State Trail over Manning Avenue. (The Register list dates it 1877; Wikipedia and MnDOT give 1873.)',
  'The pin-connected iron truss with ornamental lattice bracing, its plates pierced with circles and crosses (MnDOT); a concrete deck and safety cables were added in 2011. It is on the trail itself, so you walk or ride across it.',
  'An 1873 Main Street bridge on its third river and its third life.',
  accn='Gateway State Trail (Minnesota DNR), over Manning Avenue (County Road 15). No parking lot was found close by: DNR\'s nearest named trail parking is near Lake Masterman on Highway 96 in Grant, under the trail bridge, and at Pine Point Regional Park north of Stillwater (fee). Trail hours not checked.',
  links=[W('Gateway Trail Iron Bridge'),['MnDOT: historic bridge 82524','https://www.dot.state.mn.us/historicbridges/82524.html'],['Minnesota DNR: Gateway State Trail','https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_trails/gateway/index.html']],
  brick='no'),
# ---- Scandia ----
N('hay-lake-school-erickson-house','Hay Lake School and Johannes Erickson log house','Scandia','mn','civic','standing',45.23169,-92.82033,'exact','permit','walk','','1868–1963',
  'Scandia\'s first purpose-built school, a small brick schoolhouse finished in 1896 and used until 1963. One of the men behind it, Peter Rosell, drowned when he broke through the ice of the St. Croix carrying bricks for it. Local graduates reopened it for tours in 1970, and the Washington County Historical Society took it over in 1974, moving beside it the 1868 gambrel-roofed log house of Johannes Erickson, built by Swedish immigrants who lived there until 1904.',
  'The schoolhouse with its belfry and latticed porch, with its classroom inside, and the hewn-log Erickson house (Wikipedia, WCHS). A 1900 monument to the first Swedish settlers in Minnesota stands nearby.',
  'A one-room school whose bricks cost a builder his life, beside a Swedish log house.',
  accn='Washington County Historical Society, 14020 195th Street North. WCHS says the site is open seasonally May to October and by appointment only; adults $10, ages 6–17 $3, cash or cheque (651-433-4014). Lot in Hay Lake Park beside it.',
  links=[W('Hay Lake School'),['WCHS: Hay Lake School and Erickson Log Home',WCHS+'?p=25']],
  th=[45.23229,-92.82090],thn='Hay Lake Park lot, Old Marine Trail',brick='yes'),
N('gammelgarden-scandia','Gammelgården Museum: 1856 Elim log church','Scandia','mn','worship','standing',45.25190,-92.80793,'site','open','walk','','1855–1879',
  'An open-air museum begun in 1972 by Elim Lutheran Church on its old parsonage farm. The first Elim sanctuary, built in 1856 of more than 200 hardwood logs, was the Hay Lake School from 1860, a hay barn from 1899 to 1981, and was moved here and rededicated in 1982; the museum and Wikipedia call it the oldest Lutheran church building in Minnesota. The 1868 log parsonage, the oldest Lutheran parsonage in the state, and the 1879 barn are on their own ground; an immigrant log house with an 1855 land patent came from Bone Lake.',
  'Horizontal-log buildings with notched-and-pegged corners: the 1856 church with replica pews and one original, the two-storey 1868 parsonage (closed 2024–2029 for restoration), the 1879 barn on a fieldstone lower storey built of boards sawn at the Marine mill, the 1855 immigrant house with a 12-pane window and hand-forged hinges from Sweden, and a 1930 Swedish-style cottage with its original clay tile roof. The buildings open only on guided tours and at events.',
  'Minnesota\'s oldest Lutheran church, which spent eighty years as a hay barn.',
  accn='Private nonprofit museum on Olinda Trail North. The museum says it is open from the first Saturday in May to the third week of December, Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.; building tours run May to October, and walk-in tours only Friday to Sunday. Admission not checked. Parking lot at the museum.',
  links=[W('Gammelgården Museum of Scandia'),['Gammelgården: historic buildings','https://gammelgardenmuseum.org/tours/historic-buildings/'],['Gammelgården: questions (hours)','https://gammelgardenmuseum.org/tours/faqs/']],
  brick='no'),
]
